                    Catholic moral teaching does not begin with the suspicion that desire itself is evil. It begins with a simple observation: whenever a person chooses deliberately, that person is reaching for something presented as good. We may seek a genuinely good thing, misunderstand what would be good, or choose a small good in a way that injures a greater one. Even a sinful choice normally attracts us through some fragment of apparent good—relief, pleasure, control, approval, belonging, or escape. The moral problem is not that the will desires good; it is that our judgment and desire can become confused about what the good really is and how different goods belong together.

                    This is why the Christian moral life is more than compliance. A rule can tell us a boundary, but virtue educates the person who must perceive, desire, judge, and act within that boundary. Consider work. Meaningful labor, competence, provision for a family, and service to society are real goods. Yet career success becomes disordered when it is asked to provide a person's entire identity, justify neglect of solemn promises, or excuse dishonesty. The problem is not that the career is worthless. The problem is that a limited good has been treated as final.

                    The Catechism places the desire for happiness at the beginning of its treatment of morality because that desire is planted in the human heart by God. We need not extinguish the desire to be happy. We need it purified and led home. Moral formation asks repeatedly: What good am I seeking? Is it truly good? What place should it occupy? Whom will my manner of seeking it help or harm? Does this choice move me toward communion with God and neighbor, or does it turn a gift into an idol?
